On the whole, I like this idea.  However, a few minor points:

>* you may deposit as much gold as you like, however the gold is credited
>    to the _person_ named on the escutcheon. a name change will cut you
>    off from your money. escutcheons fade and your account disappears
>    when you die. escutcheons are rare but found normally throughout
>    the dungeon. there is a slight chance that a treasurer would sell
>    you an escutcheon (1,000 gold pieces).

This, I think, would be bad...  A large and conspicuous feature like this
   should not be limited this much.  Imagine how you would feel if you
   couldn't use an altar without a holy relic, which is extremely unusual to
   find.  Bang!  Poly-piling (which I still haven't been able to do because
   I don't reroll characters (ever) and NEVER FIND WANDS OF POLYMORPH) would
   be even more important.  This is just too useful to limit like that.  Maybe
   you should have to have a blank scroll (easier to find or make), which
   becomes a "letter of credit" (tool).  (I would probably say escutcheon, but
   I have no clue what they are, and am mildly allergic to making a fool of
   myself by misusing a word.)  Or leave it a scroll.  If you have one scroll
   that can be reread an infinate number of times (blank), you can have two.

>* at the time of the deposit, you could withdraw 1 gold doubloon (a small
>    rock which cannot be created) for every 5,000 gold pieces in your
>    account. the gold doubloon has unit weight.

You have quite a list here for doubloons.  What are they FOR?  What do they
do that a letter of credit (escutcheon) or gold itself wouldn't?  If it is
just the weight difference, I don't think it is worth it.  If it is for
payment in shops and such, well, maybe...

